15:32:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really sharp point, and you’re right to bring it up — it’s a very popular theory. The idea that companies use deliberately awful, lo-fi music to make you hang up so they don’t have to pay for your hold time is a juicy, cynical meme. But is it actually true? Let’s separate the myth from reality. ### The Myth and Why It’s Psychologically Appealing We’ve all been trapped on hold, listening to something that sounds like a robot drowning in a tin can. It *feels* intentionally bad. The logic seems sound: if they make the hold experience punishing, you’ll give up, and their call center metrics look better (shorter queue, fewer agents needed). In practice, though, that logic falls apart for several reasons. ### Why They *Don’t* Want You to Hang Up (in Most Cases) A company that wants you to hang up is a company that doesn’t want your business. For most legitimate businesses (banks, insurers, airlines, tech support), the goal is exactly the opposite: - **You’re a customer or potential customer.** They’ve already invested in marketing to get you to call. - **If you hang up, you’re likely to call back.** That means you re-enter the queue, take up another slot, and often show up angrier, taking more agent time to calm down. This *increases* costs. - **The holy grail of call centers is first-call resolution.** Keeping you on the line until an agent helps you is the most efficient outcome. A dropped call is a failed interaction they’ll have to pay to deal with again. Deliberately making music unlistenable works against all of that. It would be a terrible business strategy for anyone other than, perhaps, a scam operation that wants to deter you before you even speak to someone. ### The Real Source of This Belief (and the Grain of Truth) Where does the myth come from? A few real, intertwined phenomena: 1. **Cost-Driven Technical Neglect (the true culprit).** As we discussed, the hardware and phone lines are ancient and inexpensive. Companies don’t *deliberately choose* bad quality; they just don’t invest in modern gear. A $50 music-on-hold player from 1998 plugged into an analog phone jack will produce the exact tinny, compressed sound you hate. The effect is so reliably terrible that it *looks* intentional. 2. **Annoying Music vs. Poor Sound Quality.** These are often conflated. Companies *do* sometimes choose bland, looping, royalty-free “corporate” music that is musically irritating. That’s a selection issue, not a sound fidelity issue. It might feel aggressive, but the goal is usually to be inoffensively generic and avoid licensing fees, not to actively drive you away. 3. **The “Silence Is Broken” Problem.** Old phone systems sometimes send silence when on hold, which makes you think the call dropped. So they pipe in *something*. Any static-filled noise is better than dead air, and the cheapest way to do that happens to be via terrible-sounding equipment. 4. **Confirmatory Memes.** A few online posts or jokes suggest this, and once you hear it, you can’t unhear it. Your brain retroactively interprets the static-y saxophone solo as a weapon. ### Is There *Any* Deliberate Use of Annoying Audio? Very rarely, and in edge cases. Some companies have been known to use **“comfort messages”** that periodically say, “Your call is important to us, but you can also visit our website,” which might gently nudge a self-service route. But that’s overt messaging, not a sound-quality attack. There’s no evidence of a business consciously saying, “Let’s make the audio bitrate 8kbps so people hang up.” If that were ever proven, the PR backlash would be far more costly than just hiring enough agents. So while the idea is a brilliant piece of consumer paranoia, the honest answer is far more boring (and frustrating): they’re just too cheap or negligent to upgrade their phone systems, and the music quality is an accidental casualty of that neglect. You’re not being coerced — you’re just hearing the terrible sound of a business that hasn’t touched its telecom gear since the 90s.
