ith cheesy synths. When you combine a gratingly generic lift-music jazz loop with the narrowband phone filter, the result feels actively hostile. The choice of the cheesy music can be a deliberate artistic (or cost-saving) decision, but the *muffled, static-y, 8-kHz nightmare quality* is not. They didn’t say “make it worse”; they just picked the cheapest music and played it through the cheapest hardware. ### 3. The “Purposeful Pain” Theory Falls Apart Under Scrutiny Let’s test your theory: if a company truly wanted to weaponize bad audio to make you hang up, they would… do what, exactly? They already control the volume. They could blast a 1 kHz tone at ear-splitting level, or play literal baby cries. Instead, they play music. Poor-quality music, yes, but music. The fact that it’s *music* at all — an attempt to fill silence — suggests the intent is to soothe or distract, not to assault. If their goal were call abandonment, silence would be far more effective: with completely dead air, 90% of callers would assume the line dropped and hang up within seconds. But they don’t do that, because they don’t want you to hang up. ### The Counterexample That Helps Prove It Notice that when you’re on hold with a company that uses modern VoIP with HD Voice (some tech companies, newer insurance firms, or if you’re calling via an app like WhatsApp or FaceTime Audio to a business that supports it), the hold music suddenly sounds… fine? It might still be bland, but it’s clear, full, with bass and treble intact. The difference is night and day. The music didn’t become less annoying on purpose; the transmission pipe just got wider, proving the fault was the pipe, not the source. So, your feeling is 100% valid: it’s an infuriating, tinny, universally loathed experience. But rather than a calculated act of psychological warfare, it’s a perfect storm of neglected infrastructure, cost-cutting, and a century-old phone network that simply doesn’t care about music. You’re all hearing the same terrible sound because you’re all using the same terrible, unbudging technology — not because every company on Earth secretly plotted in unison to annoy you off the line.
